<Base title="Can search & AI systems understand your business?" description="Six practical website checks covering crawl access, readable content, clear services, business identity, evidence and meaningful measurement.">
<article><header class="page-hero wrap"><h1>Can AI understand<br /><em>your business?</em></h1><p class="lead">Start with six practical checks: access, readable content, specific services, consistent identity, credible evidence and a repeatable way to measure what happens.</p><p>By <a href="/about/">SE LABS</a> · <time datetime="2026-10-01">1 October 2026</time></p></header>
<div class="article-grid section wrap"><aside><nav aria-label="In this guide"><a href="#access">1. Access</a><a href="#content">2. Readable content</a><a href="#services">3. Specific services</a><a href="#identity">4. Business identity</a><a href="#evidence">5. Credible evidence</a><a href="#measurement">6. Measurement</a></nav></aside><div class="prose"><h2 id="access">1. Can the page be accessed?</h2><p>A public page needs to load successfully without requiring login. Check your robots.txt, page indexing directives and hosting rules. Allowing a crawler in one place does not help if a firewall blocks it elsewhere.</p><p>OpenAI uses OAI-SearchBot for search and GPTBot for training-related crawling. Their controls are independent. See <a href="https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/bots">OpenAI’s crawler documentation</a>.</p><h2 id="content">2. Is the important content readable?</h2><p>Check the HTML response, not only a screenshot. Your business description, services and relevant links should be actual text. Static rendering is one way to deliver that content without depending on client-side JavaScript.</p><h2 id="services">3. Does each service explain a real offer?</h2><p>A label such as “growth” is not enough on its own. Explain who the service helps, the problem it addresses, what the work includes and how to take the next step. Give substantial services their own pages and link them from the main navigation or service directory.</p><h2 id="identity">4. Is the business identity consistent?</h2><p>Use a consistent business name, location and service description. Check the profiles you control. If you add structured data, make sure it agrees with what visitors can read; it should not introduce unsupported claims.</p><h2 id="evidence">5. Can a reader check your claims?</h2><p>A useful case study describes the starting point, work performed, measurement period and limitations. Explain which data came from your own work. Publish customer details only when you have permission. A clear method is useful, but it must not be presented as a measured result.</p><h2 id="measurement">6. What would count as progress?</h2><p>Use a consistent set of customer questions and record the exact responses, dates and citations. Count mentions and links separately. Repeat observations and connect them to qualified enquiries where attribution is available.</p><h2>What to do first</h2><p>Fix access and unclear information before expanding the site. Google says that its AI features use existing SEO foundations; there is no special AI markup required. Read <a href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/appearance/ai-features">Google’s official guidance</a>.</p><p>Our recommendation is to make the business easy to understand, back it with evidence and measure a specific set of questions. No file, framework or checklist guarantees inclusion.</p><a class="text-link" href="/lab/">Explore our approach ↗</a></div></div></article>
</Base>
